NEW  <p> Where to start: Supavit Thavaraputta's article, "The 1868 Solar Eclipse: Some Considerations about Its Sources and Historiography", Thammasat Journal of History 8(2), 2021, pages 34 to 57 (its page at the journal), is a research article on the Thai sources for this eclipse and on how its history has been written; it takes up exactly this question on pages 46 to 48 and would be the place to start.
OLD  <p> It is in Thai, with an English abstract.
NEW  <p> What I have not done: I have read one revision of the article and not its history, so I cannot say how the sentence came into it.
OLD  <p> I have not seen the newspaper itself, and the page does not say what the newspaper's writer rested on; the passage does quote twice from the diary of "the Rev.
OLD  <p> Dan Beach Bradley", and the sentence with the two seconds is outside both quotations, in the writer's own words.
NEW  <p> ↑ Back to the list of notes
OLD  <h2> What none of the notes covers
OLD  <p> I have not read the talk pages of the three articles or their archives, so some of this may already have been raised there.
OLD  <p> Nothing on this page is a source: an edit should rest on the articles' own sources, read by the editor who makes it.
